<%- include('../partials/head', { title: 'Admin - Repos', user: user }) %>
  <main class="content admin-content">
    <%- include('partials/nav', { active: 'repos' }) %>
    <h1>Repos</h1>
    <% if (error) { %><p class="error"><%= error %></p><% } %>
    <%# The label has to say what this gate is NOT. Three independent gates
        govern documents and none subsumes another: doc_definitions.is_active is
        "should this document exist anywhere", this flag is "may users run
        documents against this repo's branches", and the maintenance lock is "is
        the system able to run anything right now". Reading this checkbox as
        "documents off for this repo" would be wrong - the nightly trunk
        document keeps arriving either way (see ROADMAP.md Phase 25). %>
    <p class="placeholder">
      <strong>On-demand runs</strong> lets anyone with access to a repo run one of the documents
      defined on <a href="/admin/documents">Documents</a> against a branch, from that branch's page,
      and read the result immediately. It does not affect the nightly documents: those keep
      regenerating from the repo's default branch whether this is on or off. Turn it off for a repo
      whose branch diffs are too large to review usefully, or whose model spend needs containing.
    </p>
    <%# The state ladder, stated once here rather than per row. Each step down
        removes exactly one capability, which is what makes the control
        explainable without a table in front of you (see ROADMAP.md Phase 27). %>
    <p class="placeholder">
      <strong>State</strong> is a repo's lifecycle, and no state ever hides it: anyone with access to
      the group keeps seeing and reading every repo in it. <strong>Enabled</strong> is everything.
      <strong>Read-only</strong> keeps the repo readable and its documents current &mdash; nightly and
      on demand &mdash; but starts no new branch or pipeline work. <strong>Disabled</strong> keeps
      everything readable and generates nothing: documents stay frozen at the commit they were last
      written from, and the repo costs no GitHub calls and no model spend overnight. The note is shown
      to engineers on the repo's own pages and is the answer to "why can't I create a branch here".
    </p>
    <%# The two ways to retire a repo are on this same row, and they are not
        alternatives - delete is the removal verb and Disabled is explicitly not
        one (see ROADMAP.md Phase 28). Saying so here is what stops an admin
        picking the irreversible one meaning the reversible one. %>
    <p class="placeholder">
      <strong>Disabled is how a repo is retired. Delete is not.</strong> Delete removes the row itself and
      only succeeds on a repo nothing references &mdash; one added by mistake and never used. The moment a
      repo has change orders, branches, runs or documents, deleting it would destroy the record those
      tables exist to keep, so it is refused and the refusal names how much history is in the way.
      Setting State to <strong>Disabled</strong> is the retirement that keeps all of it readable.
    </p>
    <table class="admin-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Repo Group</th><th>Name</th><th>Description</th><th>Default Branch</th><th>State</th><th class="row-danger-cell">Remove</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <%# Disabled repos sort last (see the ORDER BY in repos.js) and recede
            here, so a frozen repo stops competing for attention with live ones
            without becoming hard to find. %>
        <% repos.forEach(function(repo) { %>
        <tr<%= repo.access_state === 'disabled' ? ' class="repo-row-frozen"' : '' %>>
          <td colspan="4">
            <form method="POST" action="/admin/repos/<%= repo.id %>/update" class="row-form">
              <select name="repo_group_id">
                <% repoGroups.forEach(function(rg) { %>
                <option value="<%= rg.id %>" <%= rg.id === repo.repo_group_id ? 'selected' : '' %>><%= rg.org_name %> / <%= rg.name %></option>
                <% }) %>
              </select>
              <input type="text" name="name" value="<%= repo.name %>" required>
              <input type="text" name="description" value="<%= repo.description || '' %>" placeholder="Description">
              <input type="text" name="default_branch_name" value="<%= repo.default_branch_name %>" placeholder="main">
              <label class="checkbox-label">
                <input type="checkbox" name="on_demand_docs_enabled" value="1" <%= repo.on_demand_docs_enabled ? 'checked' : '' %>>
                On-demand runs
              </label>
              <button type="submit">Save</button>
            </form>
          </td>
          <td>
            <%# Separate form from the row save above, matching its own route:
                freezing a repo and renaming one are different operations and
                should not have to happen together.

                The confirm is only armed when there is queued work to lose sight
                of. A queued session on a non-enabled repo stays queued and is
                skipped rather than failed, so it runs again the moment the repo
                is enabled - the hazard is that it sits invisibly in the
                meantime, which is why the count is on the row as well. %>
            <form method="POST" action="/admin/repos/<%= repo.id %>/access-state" class="row-form"
              <% if (repo.queued_sessions) { %>onsubmit="return this.access_state.value === 'enabled' || confirm('<%= repo.queued_sessions %> queued run(s) on this repo will not start while it is not enabled. They stay queued and run once it is enabled again. Continue?')"<% } %>>
              <select name="access_state">
                <% accessStates.forEach(function(s) { %>
                <option value="<%= s.value %>" <%= s.value === repo.access_state ? 'selected' : '' %>><%= s.label %></option>
                <% }) %>
              </select>
              <input type="text" name="access_state_note" value="<%= repo.access_state_note || '' %>"
                placeholder="Why (shown to engineers)" maxlength="500">
              <button type="submit">Set state</button>
            </form>
            <% if (repo.access_state !== 'enabled' && repo.queued_sessions) { %>
              <p class="placeholder">
                <span class="health-badge health-warning"><%= repo.queued_sessions %> queued</span>
                not starting while this repo is <%= repo.access_state === 'read_only' ? 'read-only' : 'disabled' %>
              </p>
            <% } %>
          </td>
          <%# Set apart from the rest of the row - its own cell, its own rule,
              its own confirm - for the reason Phase 26 moved Delete off the
              documents list: the routine controls and the irreversible one
              should not read as two spellings of the same action. The confirm
              describes the limit rather than asking "are you sure", since the
              honest answer for a referenced repo is that this will refuse. It
              deliberately does not name the repo: an admin-entered name holding
              an apostrophe would break the handler's JS string, and a confirm
              that fails to parse is a Delete that fires with no confirm at all
              (the hazard models.ejs already notes). %>
          <td class="row-danger-cell">
            <form method="POST" action="/admin/repos/<%= repo.id %>/delete" class="row-form"
              onsubmit="return confirm('Delete this repo row? This is not how a repo is retired - it only succeeds if nothing references it, and it cannot be undone. To retire a repo with history, set its State to Disabled instead.')">
              <button type="submit" class="danger-button">Delete</button>
            </form>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <% }) %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h2>New repo</h2>
    <form method="POST" action="/admin/repos" class="stacked-form">
      <label>Repo Group
        <select name="repo_group_id" required>
          <option value="" disabled selected>Select a repo group</option>
          <% repoGroups.forEach(function(rg) { %>
          <option value="<%= rg.id %>"><%= rg.org_name %> / <%= rg.name %></option>
          <% }) %>
        </select>
      </label>
      <label>Name <input type="text" name="name" required></label>
      <label>Description <input type="text" name="description"></label>
      <label>Default branch <input type="text" name="default_branch_name" placeholder="main"></label>
      <button type="submit">Create</button>
    </form>
  </main>
<%- include('../partials/foot') %>
